static.solution:
{e}^{3 x+4 y}={e}^{3 x}\cdot {e}^{4 y}  to compute the double integral as the product of two single integrals. This gives



\begin{array}{rcl}
\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{R}} e^{3 x + 4 y}\,dA\amp =\amp 
\int_0^{9}\int_0^{4}{e}^{3 x}{e}^{4 y} \,dx \,dy\\
\amp =\amp \left(\int_0^{4}{e}^{3 x} \,dx\right)\left(\int_0^{9}{e}^{4 y} \,dy\right)\\
\amp =\amp \left(\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3 x}\bigg|_0^{4}\right)\left(\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4 y}\bigg|_0^{9}\right)\\
\amp =\amp \frac{1}{3}\left({e}^{12}-{e}^0\right)\cdot \frac{1}{4}\left({e}^{36}-{e}^0\right)\\
\amp =\amp \frac{1}{12}\left({e}^{12}-1\right)\left({e}^{36}-1\right)
\approx 5.84724\times 10^{19}
\end{array}
